1
00:00:01,700 --> 00:00:06,820
That is it for stores. Also check out the lecture after this lecture where I also

2
00:00:06,820 --> 00:00:09,200
prepared some additional things you can do with stores.

3
00:00:09,480 --> 00:00:15,140
But the main thing about stores is that you can create writable and readable stores.

4
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:20,700
Readable stores are great for pieces of data that update automatically.

5
00:00:21,220 --> 00:00:26,300
Writable stores will be the main stores you interact with because they allow you to both

6
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:27,260
subscribe to them

7
00:00:27,260 --> 00:00:29,820
and trigger changes in the data

8
00:00:29,820 --> 00:00:32,320
manually based on user events and so on.

9
00:00:32,580 --> 00:00:34,680
And you also learned about custom stores,

10
00:00:34,980 --> 00:00:38,180
which is a useful enhancement for writable stores,

11
00:00:38,180 --> 00:00:41,840
where you can hide away some of the complexity

12
00:00:41,840 --> 00:00:43,100
from your components

13
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:45,140
and put it into the store file instead

14
00:00:45,140 --> 00:00:46,900
and have all your heavy lifting,

15
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:48,680
all your logic in the store file.

16
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:49,680
And in the components,

17
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:53,220
you can just call easy to use methods like remove item

18
00:00:53,220 --> 00:00:55,300
and all the magic happens behind the scenes.

19
00:00:55,300 --> 00:01:02,120
Stores are super useful features in Svelte because they allow you to outsource logic and most

20
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:05,140
importantly data management into dedicated files.

21
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:10,280
They make sure that you don't have infinitely long prop and custom event chains.

22
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:17,660
And they particularly make communicating between loosely related components like the product and the card component

23
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:19,100
here much easier.

